WEBVTT

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:01.860
GERALD VERSLUIS: Hello and
welcome to this session

00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:05.670
about taking your AI to
the edge with.NET MAUI.

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:07.950
My name is Gerald and I
work here at Microsoft

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:10.950
on the.NET team, specifically
on the.NET MAUI team.

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:13.320
And in this session, we're going
to see what the current state

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:15.960
of MAUI is in.NET 10,
what we've been building,

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:20.580
what to expect from.NET 11, but
also how we as a team became

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:23.370
so much more productive
with the use of Copilot,

00:00:23.370 --> 00:00:26.580
how you can be more productive
building your.NET MAUI apps

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:28.740
with the tools that
we're putting out there,

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:32.550
and how you can deliver that
value to your end customer.

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:34.140
So let's dig in.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:36.690
For a quick reminder, quick
refresher for the people

00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:38.370
who might be new seeing this,

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:40.830
what is this.NET
MAUI thing again?

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:44.700
.NET MAUI stands for.NET
Multiplatform App UI,

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:46.170
and that is our offering

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:51.180
to build cross-platform amazing
looking apps on iOS, Android,

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:54.630
Windows, and macOS
through C Sharp and.NET.

00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:57.660
So we have single project,
single code base.

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:00.960
So you just have a single
project to launch all of these,

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:03.450
to reach all of these projects,
all of these platforms,

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:04.860
which is really, really amazing.

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:07.800
It all maps to native
controls and native styling

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:10.830
on the platform exactly as you
would expect on the platform

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:11.870
that you're running on.

00:01:11.870 --> 00:01:14.870
It feels and looks exactly
as it's supposed to be

00:01:14.870 --> 00:01:18.200
on the operating system, on the
device that you're running on.

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:21.770
We have, like, all kinds of
devices, APIs you can reach

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:24.140
with that unified C
Sharp and.NET layer.

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:27.020
You can get the geolocation,
you can get the flashlights,

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:29.570
you can get the file system,
all these kinds of things,

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:33.590
all built in, and lots and
lots of controls already.

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:36.350
So everything you need to
build cross-platform apps

00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:40.260
that are useful for everyone.

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:43.560
Then we also have something
that is called Blazor Hybrid.

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:47.400
And with that, you can take
your existing Blazor offering,

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:49.140
your existing
Blazor application,

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:52.470
put that in a Blazor Hybrid
WebView, and then package

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:55.200
that as a mobile app and
put that in the stores,

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:58.380
have that little icon on the
screen, without having to lose

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:01.290
or reinvent anything, because
you can just take your existing

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:04.740
Blazor app, or if you just have
this one very specific Blazor

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:07.980
control that you have, that
you've created, and you want

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:09.960
to reuse that in
your.NET MAUI app,

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:11.640
you can absolutely
do that as well.

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:12.660
The choice is yours.

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:14.640
We have options for everyone.

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:17.940
You can make your full app a
hybrid application with Blazor.

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:19.380
You can just have
this little part

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:22.790
of your application be a little
Blazor Hybrid application,

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:24.020
and the rest is fully native,

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:25.460
and it will integrate
seamlessly.

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:27.350
You will not be able to
tell the difference.

00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:29.360
And if you want to take
it even a step further,

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:32.450
and you maybe have like a
React Native, an Angular,

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:35.030
or some other
JavaScript application,

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:36.710
we have something
for that as well.

00:02:36.710 --> 00:02:39.290
We have the hybrid
WebView there too.

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:41.900
So lots and lots of
options for everyone,

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:46.470
depending on where you are in
your development lifecycle,

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:47.490
what you want to do there.

00:02:47.490 --> 00:02:53.430
So the.NET ecosystem has this
rich choice for every developer,

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:54.960
for every audience, right?

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:57.000
If you want to go for reach,
then you probably want to go

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:59.040
with Blazor, like Blazor
in the web browser.

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:00.570
Everything that
runs a web browser,

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:02.880
you can take your
Blazor app there.

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:06.870
If you want to full power the
native power on actual devices,

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:09.180
then you probably want
to stick with.NET MAUI,

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:10.440
where you can do all of that.

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:14.160
And you can kind of like
come in between with Blazor

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:16.620
and.NET MAUI together,
which is a very,

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:17.940
very powerful combination.

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:19.890
And you can reach
all of these things

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:22.440
with your existing web
development experience

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:24.780
and whatever you have there.

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:26.250
Also, customers love this.

00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:29.070
Customers love building
with our product.

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:33.000
We have had so many customer
stories on our website.

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:34.740
The link will be down below.

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:36.480
And I just wanted to
highlight this one,

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:38.580
which is an amazing
one, Moti Me.

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:40.170
They're based in the UK,

00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:45.390
and they have these amazing
pediatric physiotherapy devices

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:49.560
to support kind of like their
work with those pediatricians.

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:53.430
And that is powered with a.NET
MAUI companion application,

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:57.330
which I personally love that
our tech, our stuff is used

00:03:57.330 --> 00:03:59.130
for stuff that
actually, you know,

00:03:59.130 --> 00:04:00.840
does something there
out in the world.

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:03.210
Of course, all your
applications are amazing

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:05.010
and doing something great
out there in the world.

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:07.590
But these things, like
the little helper things

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:10.710
for the medical space, I
absolutely love those.

00:04:10.710 --> 00:04:13.890
So go check out the full
customer story on our website.

00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:15.960
The link is down there
below and will be

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:18.030
in the video resources here.

00:04:18.030 --> 00:04:21.380
It is really, really amazing.

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:24.770
So I already said, like, hey,
we have been, as a team,

00:04:24.770 --> 00:04:28.580
as a.NET team at Microsoft,
specifically the.NET MAUI team,

00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:32.000
we have been changing the
way we work drastically

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:33.710
in the last couple of months.

00:04:33.710 --> 00:04:34.890
And it shows.

00:04:34.890 --> 00:04:35.850
It shows, right?

00:04:35.850 --> 00:04:40.740
We have incorporated Copilot
and AI fully end-to-end

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:43.620
in our software development
lifecycle, right?

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:46.410
So we now build with
Copilot everywhere,

00:04:46.410 --> 00:04:48.570
not just code generation,
not just code generation

00:04:48.570 --> 00:04:52.680
on our devices to write code
faster, more code for.NET MAUI.

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:53.790
Absolutely not.

00:04:53.790 --> 00:04:57.870
But also to reproduce the
things that you might report

00:04:57.870 --> 00:04:59.610
on the GitHub repository, right?

00:04:59.610 --> 00:05:02.130
It's an open-source project.

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:04.650
So you can go there,
you can report issues.

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:07.260
And sometimes, you know,
it's hard to reproduce.

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:09.480
We always encourage you
to reproduce it yourself.

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:12.000
But now we have a little
helper that's called Copilot.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:15.180
And we can just say, hey, can
you try and reproduce this thing

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:17.730
that this customer
is reporting and see

00:05:17.730 --> 00:05:19.410
if we can work with that?

00:05:19.410 --> 00:05:20.760
Of course, also testing, right?

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:23.550
If we have the reproduction
thing, we can also test there.

00:05:23.550 --> 00:05:27.750
So not just running the test,
we have thousands and thousands

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:31.110
of UI tests on the.NET
MAUI repository.

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:34.710
So you can imagine those
take a little while to run.

00:05:34.710 --> 00:05:36.150
Now we can be smarter with that.

00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:38.700
We can say to Copilot, hey,
figure out for the change

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:42.090
in this PR or this bug, what
are the relevant UI tests?

00:05:42.090 --> 00:05:44.760
Only run those and give
us the results, right?

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:47.250
And it will just take -- well,
it still takes a little bit

00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:49.920
of time, but at least we don't
have to spend time on it

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:53.880
to figure out which ones are
the relevant ones here and wait

00:05:53.880 --> 00:05:55.830
for the results, et
cetera, et cetera.

00:05:55.830 --> 00:05:58.050
We can all outsource
that to Copilot.

00:05:58.050 --> 00:05:59.580
Fix, of course, like
code generation,

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:02.550
whenever there's a bug, we can
say, hey, assign to Copilot

00:06:02.550 --> 00:06:04.890
and it will go off
and do things.

00:06:04.890 --> 00:06:07.530
Review, we don't just
code generate with it,

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:10.140
but we also have
MAUI-aware reviewers.

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:13.080
So if you know a little bit
about the MAUI product,

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:15.690
.NET MAUI is a super
complex code base, right?

00:06:15.690 --> 00:06:18.480
We have all kinds of
APIs that are not native

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:23.040
to the.NET ecosystem from
iOS, from Android coming in.

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:25.770
So we have lots of things to
kind of like be aware of.

00:06:25.770 --> 00:06:30.120
So early on, we started writing
our MAUI-aware code reviewer,

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:32.370
and that is in our
pipelines as well.

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:33.900
So that will look
at a piece of code

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:35.550
and know a little
bit beyond kind

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:37.710
of like the vanilla
Copilot, if you will,

00:06:37.710 --> 00:06:39.870
that is built into GitHub.

00:06:39.870 --> 00:06:42.480
And it has a lot
of MAUI expertise

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:44.700
so that it can tell us like,
hey, look out for this,

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:47.160
look out for that, and that
we have some confidence

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:49.500
about the reviews that
are going on here.

00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:51.330
Of course, like we're
still checking in,

00:06:51.330 --> 00:06:53.220
we're still the
human in the loop,

00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:56.310
but this Copilot reviewer
has been a lot of help.

00:06:56.310 --> 00:06:58.620
Same with verification
and learning.

00:06:58.620 --> 00:07:01.530
Whenever something good
or bad happens to a PR,

00:07:01.530 --> 00:07:04.980
we can point a Copilot custom
agent to it and say like,

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:06.570
what can we learn from this PR?

00:07:06.570 --> 00:07:08.010
What happened here?

00:07:08.010 --> 00:07:10.560
And then we can take that
learning into the next PR

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:13.320
and make sure that something
doesn't happen again.

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:16.380
So we have been like
fully end-to-end

00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:19.170
into the agentic
engineering right here,

00:07:19.170 --> 00:07:22.050
all the way from like the things
that are probably obvious,

00:07:22.050 --> 00:07:24.900
writing code, reviewing
code, testing code,

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:26.670
but why not take it a
step further, right?

00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:29.700
Why not also, whenever
we merge a PR,

00:07:29.700 --> 00:07:32.460
have a Copilot agentic
workflow, look at that

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:35.970
and determine whether that
needs a documentation update,

00:07:35.970 --> 00:07:36.750
yes or no?

00:07:36.750 --> 00:07:39.720
Would it need a blog post to
highlight to people, right?

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:40.800
It will figure that out for us.

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:43.410
And not just that, it
will also open an issue

00:07:43.410 --> 00:07:46.350
and even draft a PR for
the documentation update

00:07:46.350 --> 00:07:48.090
and for that blog post.

00:07:48.090 --> 00:07:49.500
So super, super helpful.

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:52.080
That allows us to
go so much faster

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:54.240
and that shows up
in the numbers.

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:56.520
Again, we're an
open-source project,

00:07:56.520 --> 00:07:58.590
so we love to do things
out in the open.

00:07:58.590 --> 00:08:01.890
All of this you can find
on agenticengineers.net.

00:08:01.890 --> 00:08:02.880
Definitely go there.

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:06.780
You can see per
PR, on a PR basis,

00:08:06.780 --> 00:08:09.420
on the.NET MAUI repositories,
but also, of course,

00:08:09.420 --> 00:08:13.050
our supporting products, Android
and iOS, and then a bunch

00:08:13.050 --> 00:08:18.450
of tools there as well, is code
generated, is a PR authored

00:08:18.450 --> 00:08:21.750
by Copilot, or is the reviewer,

00:08:21.750 --> 00:08:23.940
the Copilot reviewer
used for this PR?

00:08:23.940 --> 00:08:25.800
And you can see, kind of
like we started picking

00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:27.060
up steam in November.

00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:29.370
That is, at least for me, kind
of like the turning point,

00:08:29.370 --> 00:08:31.710
and I think for the rest
of our team as well.

00:08:31.710 --> 00:08:34.500
November, December, January,
February, March, April.

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:38.130
April was like, you know,
our high point so far.

00:08:38.130 --> 00:08:44.540
And we have gone from 7% to 62%
Copilot-authored merged PRs

00:08:44.540 --> 00:08:45.590
in under a year, right?

00:08:45.590 --> 00:08:46.370
It's crazy.

00:08:46.370 --> 00:08:49.760
That allows us to bring
value to you so much faster,

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:51.950
all the bug fixes, all
the new functionality

00:08:51.950 --> 00:08:54.410
that we didn't really
get to before.

00:08:54.410 --> 00:08:58.010
Now we have the tools to kind
of like go there, do that much,

00:08:58.010 --> 00:09:01.880
much faster, and produce all
that extra value for you

00:09:01.880 --> 00:09:05.450
so that we have this
amazing product.

00:09:05.450 --> 00:09:06.560
Go to that website.

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:07.970
There is lots of
more data there.

00:09:07.970 --> 00:09:10.610
And, you know, if you
have any questions there,

00:09:10.610 --> 00:09:13.490
