00:13:36.780 --> 00:13:39.630
where they are sharing their
numbers with how Copilot

00:13:39.630 --> 00:13:42.030
and AI has increased
their velocity.

00:13:42.030 --> 00:13:46.470
So you can see, like, hey, 50%
to 70% time reduction, right,

00:13:46.470 --> 00:13:48.030
on the things that
they are spending on,

00:13:48.030 --> 00:13:49.500
trying to reproduce,
trying to fix,

00:13:49.500 --> 00:13:50.730
trying to do all these things.

00:13:50.730 --> 00:13:52.230
So that they can get through two

00:13:52.230 --> 00:13:57.210
to three more times more
issues every week, more PRs,

00:13:57.210 --> 00:13:59.430
and more first-time
fixed rate, right?

00:13:59.430 --> 00:14:02.100
So it's been amazing across
the board, basically.

00:14:02.100 --> 00:14:04.230
That is our message.

00:14:04.230 --> 00:14:06.480
Then we also have Uno, right?

00:14:06.480 --> 00:14:10.590
Uno has been partnering with
us now on the SkiaSharp.

00:14:10.590 --> 00:14:13.530
Mostly, like, we've been
partnering on some more things,

00:14:13.530 --> 00:14:17.700
but now a blog post
came out for SkiaSharp,

00:14:17.700 --> 00:14:20.460
which is also maintained
at Microsoft.

00:14:20.460 --> 00:14:24.360
But now Uno has stepped
up and has invested

00:14:24.360 --> 00:14:26.790
in taking it to the next step.

00:14:26.790 --> 00:14:30.690
And I think with, like, the
new major version update

00:14:30.690 --> 00:14:32.790
in a while now.

00:14:32.790 --> 00:14:34.710
So this has been
really, really great.

00:14:34.710 --> 00:14:36.780
And thank you, Uno,
for doing that

00:14:36.780 --> 00:14:39.600
and making this
better for everyone.

00:14:39.600 --> 00:14:40.740
But it doesn't stop there.

00:14:40.740 --> 00:14:41.640
Oh, no, no, no.

00:14:41.640 --> 00:14:44.730
The.NET ecosystem, in the.NET
ecosystem, we're all friends.

00:14:44.730 --> 00:14:47.610
So Avalonia also came
up and said, hey,

00:14:47.610 --> 00:14:50.520
what if we can embed.NET
MAUI applications

00:14:50.520 --> 00:14:54.450
in our Avalonia backend, and
now we can bring MAUI to Linux

00:14:54.450 --> 00:14:55.470
and the browser, right?

00:14:55.470 --> 00:14:57.450
So Avalonia has been
doing a great job there.

00:14:57.450 --> 00:14:59.190
We've been collaborating
with them.

00:14:59.190 --> 00:15:02.340
They wrote a little blog
post about how that went

00:15:02.340 --> 00:15:04.920
and what's coming and how
to get started with this.

00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:07.680
So if this is something
that is of interest to you,

00:15:07.680 --> 00:15:09.600
then definitely go
check this out.

00:15:09.600 --> 00:15:13.080
Thank you so much, partners and
friends, for working together

00:15:13.080 --> 00:15:17.670
and making.NET the best
ecosystem there is.

00:15:17.670 --> 00:15:18.270
All right.

00:15:18.270 --> 00:15:21.330
So what did we do
for.NET MAUI 10, right?

00:15:21.330 --> 00:15:23.250
We focused on a couple
of things here, stable,

00:15:23.250 --> 00:15:24.900
fast, simple, and modern.

00:15:24.900 --> 00:15:28.260
That are kind of like the areas
that we have been investing in,

00:15:28.260 --> 00:15:30.750
that we have been
focused on for.NET 10,

00:15:30.750 --> 00:15:33.390
which has been released
November 2025, right?

00:15:33.390 --> 00:15:35.490
The new major version of.NET.

00:15:35.490 --> 00:15:36.750
And these have been the area.

00:15:36.750 --> 00:15:39.300
So quality, write quality
is super important

00:15:39.300 --> 00:15:41.160
for something like.NET MAUI.

00:15:41.160 --> 00:15:42.990
You want to make sure
that whenever you upgrade

00:15:42.990 --> 00:15:45.930
that we do not break
anything for you.

00:15:45.930 --> 00:15:48.210
So the quality has been
top of mind for us

00:15:48.210 --> 00:15:50.070
and fixing more bugs than ever.

00:15:50.070 --> 00:15:50.970
Performance, right?

00:15:50.970 --> 00:15:54.150
We're going to -- we've
continuously been focusing

00:15:54.150 --> 00:15:59.190
on performance so that because
mobile apps like end users are

00:15:59.190 --> 00:16:03.030
like the most critical
users there are, right?

00:16:03.030 --> 00:16:06.030
If it doesn't start up fast
enough, if it's like sluggish

00:16:06.030 --> 00:16:07.860
or whatever, they will
see, they will know,

00:16:07.860 --> 00:16:08.880
they will let you know, right?

00:16:08.880 --> 00:16:10.950
So performance is always
top of mind of us.

00:16:10.950 --> 00:16:13.830
We're always trying to figure
out how to make that better.

00:16:13.830 --> 00:16:18.030
Simplicity, it should be
simple to use these APIs

00:16:18.030 --> 00:16:19.560
so that you can
implement new things.

00:16:19.560 --> 00:16:21.000
And modern, right?

00:16:21.000 --> 00:16:24.630
New Android versions, new iOS
versions come out all the time.

00:16:24.630 --> 00:16:27.420
So we want to make sure that
we are using the modern APIs

00:16:27.420 --> 00:16:29.250
so that you will see those show

00:16:29.250 --> 00:16:32.580
up in your MAUI apps
automatically as well.

00:16:32.580 --> 00:16:33.810
Also, as part of that,

00:16:33.810 --> 00:16:36.750
we've done a big
investment in XAML, right?

00:16:36.750 --> 00:16:39.390
So XAML, you know it probably
if you've been working

00:16:39.390 --> 00:16:43.170
with.NET MAUI or any other
UI framework at Microsoft.

00:16:43.170 --> 00:16:43.710
You can love it.

00:16:43.710 --> 00:16:44.310
You can hate it.

00:16:44.310 --> 00:16:46.230
But it is here to stay.

00:16:46.230 --> 00:16:49.950
And we have done some
major improvements there,

00:16:49.950 --> 00:16:53.550
some major innovations to make
it easier to work with that.

00:16:53.550 --> 00:16:55.800
So we have introduced
global namespaces

00:16:55.800 --> 00:16:57.270
so that you can reduce

00:16:57.270 --> 00:17:00.390
or completely eliminate
the XMLNS declarations

00:17:00.390 --> 00:17:01.500
in your project.

00:17:01.500 --> 00:17:03.450
We'll see that on the next
slide in a little bit.

00:17:03.450 --> 00:17:06.870
Implicit namespace is
in preview right now.

00:17:06.870 --> 00:17:10.740
In.NET 10, if you've seen
this announcement at.NET Conf

00:17:10.740 --> 00:17:15.120
in November, then it said,
hey, the language service

00:17:15.120 --> 00:17:16.980
and the editor
support is coming.

00:17:16.980 --> 00:17:19.380
We have support
now by this time.

00:17:19.380 --> 00:17:21.480
So if you want to work with
the implicit namespace,

00:17:21.480 --> 00:17:22.440
go check it out.

00:17:22.440 --> 00:17:25.470
And it should have support
now in the editor.

00:17:25.470 --> 00:17:27.600
Make sure, of course, that
if you have any feedback

00:17:27.600 --> 00:17:28.920
to report it back to us.

00:17:28.920 --> 00:17:31.860
And source generation is
really, really amazing

00:17:31.860 --> 00:17:33.750
where all the XMLNS is taken

00:17:33.750 --> 00:17:36.690
and through Roslyn
is source generated

00:17:36.690 --> 00:17:39.570
into actual C Sharp
source, right?

00:17:39.570 --> 00:17:42.810
Which has great performance
benefits, but also makes it

00:17:42.810 --> 00:17:44.940
so much easier to do
other cool stuff.

00:17:44.940 --> 00:17:46.320
So definitely go check that out.

00:17:46.320 --> 00:17:48.300
For now, opt in into.NET 10,

00:17:48.300 --> 00:17:51.420
but it will be the
default in.NET 11.

00:17:51.420 --> 00:17:54.210
So make sure to test it
right now on your project.

00:17:54.210 --> 00:17:55.980
Let us know the feedback
so that we can make sure

00:17:55.980 --> 00:18:01.410
that it's rock solid
whenever we hit.NET 11.

00:18:01.410 --> 00:18:04.290
So without global XMLNS,
right, this is even still

00:18:04.290 --> 00:18:08.820
like a small XMLNS -- sorry,
XAML document, right?

00:18:08.820 --> 00:18:11.640
But if you enable global XMLNS,

00:18:11.640 --> 00:18:14.180
then you can just take
all of this out, right?

00:18:14.180 --> 00:18:16.760
It will become so
much less verbose.

00:18:16.760 --> 00:18:19.850
So this is something, if you
ask me, really, really great

00:18:19.850 --> 00:18:23.330
so that we can get rid of
some XAML boilerplate code

00:18:23.330 --> 00:18:25.610
that we don't really
want to see anyway.

00:18:25.610 --> 00:18:27.620
XAML source generation,
I already mentioned it.

00:18:27.620 --> 00:18:29.690
So it's an opt-in
feature right now.

00:18:29.690 --> 00:18:33.380
It will have faster debug
and release performance just

00:18:33.380 --> 00:18:36.290
out of the box, just by flipping
one bit, and you don't have

00:18:36.290 --> 00:18:38.090
to worry about anything.

00:18:38.090 --> 00:18:40.100
And these numbers
are even old, right?

00:18:40.100 --> 00:18:43.590
So the way that you're using
probably right now is XAML-C,

00:18:43.590 --> 00:18:46.800
XAML compilation, which compiles
it to intermediate language.

00:18:46.800 --> 00:18:48.960
A little bit of a
technical story.

00:18:48.960 --> 00:18:52.320
But now with XAML source
generation, it will be generated

00:18:52.320 --> 00:18:54.930
to C Sharp code that
you can actually read

00:18:54.930 --> 00:18:56.190
and actually can debug.

00:18:56.190 --> 00:19:00.030
With these results, like these
numbers are not inflated,

00:19:00.030 --> 00:19:03.000
pun intended, 1,000%
faster inflation,

00:19:03.000 --> 00:19:07.200
99% less debug memory, faster
on release, less app memory.

00:19:07.200 --> 00:19:10.140
And you will have
the biggest gain

00:19:10.140 --> 00:19:11.310
in your debug builds, right?

00:19:11.310 --> 00:19:12.930
They tend to be a
little bit sluggish,

00:19:12.930 --> 00:19:14.940
especially if you do it
like on Android and have

00:19:14.940 --> 00:19:16.470
like a collection
view or whatever.

00:19:16.470 --> 00:19:18.690
It will be like
jittery and whatnot.

00:19:18.690 --> 00:19:20.700
And then you do a release
build and you're like, oh,

00:19:20.700 --> 00:19:22.980
actually my performance
issues are not that bad.

00:19:22.980 --> 00:19:25.530
That is what is going
to be fixed by this.

00:19:25.530 --> 00:19:28.020
And the other kind of like thing
that you probably notice is

00:19:28.020 --> 00:19:30.510
that we would have
differences in debug builds,

00:19:30.510 --> 00:19:33.480
in release builds, where things
would get trimmed or whatever.

00:19:33.480 --> 00:19:34.950
That is going away
entirely, right?

00:19:34.950 --> 00:19:37.770
So whenever you release
something to production,

00:19:37.770 --> 00:19:39.600
you can be safe and assured

00:19:39.600 --> 00:19:41.970
that it's the same version
as the debug version.

00:19:41.970 --> 00:19:43.590
And nothing suddenly
breaks at runtime

00:19:43.590 --> 00:19:45.450
because APIs have been taken

00:19:45.450 --> 00:19:47.490
out in the optimization
process, right?

00:19:47.490 --> 00:19:50.160
So all good things to
go around right here.

00:19:50.160 --> 00:19:52.860
Enabling right now
today is super easy.

00:19:52.860 --> 00:19:56.130
Just have this one bit
set MAUI XAML inflator

00:19:56.130 --> 00:19:57.990
to source gen, flip that bit.

00:19:57.990 --> 00:20:01.380
And then optionally, if you
want to inspect the actual code

00:20:01.380 --> 00:20:04.350
that is generated, you can do
the emit compiler generated

00:20:04.350 --> 00:20:06.930
files to true, and you
will get all the details

00:20:06.930 --> 00:20:10.410
in your favorite IDE,
in this case, VS Code.

00:20:10.410 --> 00:20:13.110
And you can see what is
going on, what is generated.

00:20:13.110 --> 00:20:14.040
Debug through there.

00:20:14.040 --> 00:20:15.360
Do all the things.

00:20:15.360 --> 00:20:18.090
Here you have a little overview
of like what that looks like.

00:20:18.090 --> 00:20:19.710
On the left, you can see XAML C

00:20:19.710 --> 00:20:22.170
and we're doing the same
operations at the same time.

00:20:22.170 --> 00:20:26.340
And you can see XAML C is taking
longer than with source gen

00:20:26.340 --> 00:20:28.620
because source gen
is flying all over,

00:20:28.620 --> 00:20:31.620
hitting all those navigation
paths and doing it much,

00:20:31.620 --> 00:20:36.030
much faster on a Pixel
5 with Android 14.

00:20:36.030 --> 00:20:39.930
Another big focus area for
us was the SafeAreas, right?

00:20:39.930 --> 00:20:42.420
iOS, I think, was first
with like the notch

00:20:42.420 --> 00:20:44.370
or the dynamic island,
all these things.

00:20:44.370 --> 00:20:46.920
And then at the bottom
with the swiping gestures.

00:20:46.920 --> 00:20:49.530
So you have little areas
that you can't really use,

00:20:49.530 --> 00:20:51.990
but your designers would
really like you to use them

00:20:51.990 --> 00:20:54.420
because it just looks so
much nicer if something goes

00:20:54.420 --> 00:20:55.890
under there and whatever.

00:20:55.890 --> 00:20:58.680
And then Android, the
devices also were there

00:20:58.680 --> 00:21:01.530
with like little camera
holes and also kind

00:21:01.530 --> 00:21:03.120
of like the little
islands or whatnot.

00:21:03.120 --> 00:21:05.460
So there's lots of
variations there right now.

00:21:05.460 --> 00:21:09.510
So it was time for us to
have a unified API rethought

00:21:09.510 --> 00:21:12.960
where we can handle all of
these scenarios better, right?

00:21:12.960 --> 00:21:15.990
So this is kind of like the
thing that I was talking about,

00:21:15.990 --> 00:21:18.090
kind of like the status
bar, I would say, roughly.

00:21:18.090 --> 00:21:20.730
And then down there with,
I don't know how to call

00:21:20.730 --> 00:21:22.550
that area, but swiping
from the bottom.

00:21:22.550 --> 00:21:24.830
Those are the areas that are
kind of like problematic.

00:21:24.830 --> 00:21:26.630
It doesn't look great
if you have like white

00:21:26.630 --> 00:21:28.280
or black boxes right there,

00:21:28.280 --> 00:21:30.800
but also you can't really put
any interactions in there.

00:21:30.800 --> 00:21:32.480
If there's a button behind
the dynamic island,

00:21:32.480 --> 00:21:33.770
that's not great too.

00:21:33.770 --> 00:21:34.850
All kinds of problems, right?

00:21:34.850 --> 00:21:35.750
Welcome to the world

00:21:35.750 --> 00:21:38.150
of cross-platform
mobile development.

00:21:38.150 --> 00:21:42.260
So it was about time to
introduce a unified-grade API

00:21:42.260 --> 00:21:44.580
that will fix all this for you.

00:21:44.580 --> 00:21:47.520
Introducing the
SafeArea edges, right?

00:21:47.520 --> 00:21:50.130
And you can set those
to none, soft input.

00:21:50.130 --> 00:21:53.070
You can set those to a couple
of different values right here

00:21:53.070 --> 00:21:56.880
to steer that behavior so that
you can make it go under,

00:21:56.880 --> 00:22:00.510
make it go not under, that
it will reserve that space.

00:22:00.510 --> 00:22:04.290
And you can set that on any
content page or any layout

00:22:04.290 --> 00:22:06.990
that you have so that you can
set per kind of like layout

00:22:06.990 --> 00:22:10.230
or whatever, if that should
go over under or how

00:22:10.230 --> 00:22:13.110
that should behave
in your application.

00:22:13.110 --> 00:22:16.590
And so you can achieve all
the great-looking designs

00:22:16.590 --> 00:22:20.310
from your app or the designer
that you are working with.

00:22:20.310 --> 00:22:23.310
Works on Android and iOS because
those are the devices that kind

00:22:23.310 --> 00:22:26.730
of like have these APIs and
these form factors right now.

00:22:26.730 --> 00:22:28.680
If something else will come
up, we will, of course,

00:22:28.680 --> 00:22:31.710
provide the implementation
for that as well.

00:22:31.710 --> 00:22:32.700
How to do that?

00:22:32.700 --> 00:22:34.410
So here we have kind
of like the default,

00:22:34.410 --> 00:22:35.820
and it's a little bit
hard to see maybe,

00:22:35.820 --> 00:22:37.800
but there at the bottom
of the screenshot,

00:22:37.800 --> 00:22:40.590
you can see the little gray
bar down there, which is kind

00:22:40.590 --> 00:22:43.620
of like, hey, grid, SafeArea
edges, none, scroll view,

00:22:43.620 --> 00:22:46.800
SafeArea edges, none, and
the content page 2, see,

00:22:46.800 --> 00:22:49.530
and you can see the
little area there

