The first controls are
already added in.NET MAUI 10

00:23:47.310 --> 00:23:48.480
through service releases,

00:23:48.480 --> 00:23:51.120
so we released the first
major initial version

00:23:51.120 --> 00:23:53.310
of.NET MAUI 10 in November.

00:23:53.310 --> 00:23:55.140
But since then,
roughly every month,

00:23:55.140 --> 00:23:58.080
we are doing service
releases with new fixes,

00:23:58.080 --> 00:24:00.090
new features where
we can, right?

00:24:00.090 --> 00:24:03.390
We're not going to
break you in any way,

00:24:03.390 --> 00:24:05.430
especially whenever API changes.

00:24:05.430 --> 00:24:08.730
But these Material 3, we
can do an opt-in, again,

00:24:08.730 --> 00:24:12.780
like one simple line in your CS
Podge, use Material 3, and boom,

00:24:12.780 --> 00:24:14.790
your Android application
will be updated

00:24:14.790 --> 00:24:18.510
to the Material 3 design
where we can, right?

00:24:18.510 --> 00:24:21.600
We have going
through the controls

00:24:21.600 --> 00:24:23.250
where we can update them.

00:24:23.250 --> 00:24:25.650
So this is going on through
the service releases,

00:24:25.650 --> 00:24:29.250
but there are some bigger
changes for some bigger layouts

00:24:29.250 --> 00:24:31.830
or whatnot going on as well.

00:24:31.830 --> 00:24:33.840
And those will be
added to.NET 11,

00:24:33.840 --> 00:24:36.060
so probably by the
time.NET 11 comes,

00:24:36.060 --> 00:24:39.750
that is where you will have the
full Material 3 experience.

00:24:39.750 --> 00:24:41.850
But I would definitely
highly encourage you

00:24:41.850 --> 00:24:43.230
to try it out today.

00:24:43.230 --> 00:24:44.700
And again, let us
know the feedback

00:24:44.700 --> 00:24:45.720
so that we can make sure

00:24:45.720 --> 00:24:48.660
that it's rock solid
whenever we hit November,

00:24:48.660 --> 00:24:53.640
and you can start
using it in day one.

00:24:53.640 --> 00:24:55.350
Service releases, I
just mentioned it.

00:24:55.350 --> 00:24:59.430
So Service Release 6 has been
our biggest service release yet.

00:24:59.430 --> 00:25:04.170
It has been 242 commits
in one service release.

00:25:04.170 --> 00:25:07.020
And service release 7 just kept
that momentum going, right?

00:25:07.020 --> 00:25:11.820
We just have more fixes,
AI-existent fixes with tests,

00:25:11.820 --> 00:25:15.120
with reviews helping us
to go so much faster.

00:25:15.120 --> 00:25:15.990
Quality, right?

00:25:15.990 --> 00:25:20.040
Collection view, carousel view,
handle the fixes, shell polish,

00:25:20.040 --> 00:25:21.780
accessibility test
coverage, right?

00:25:21.780 --> 00:25:24.450
We are adding things
across the board.

00:25:24.450 --> 00:25:26.850
Modern APIs, I didn't
even mention those.

00:25:26.850 --> 00:25:30.030
The media picker, speaking
of like modernization,

00:25:30.030 --> 00:25:33.090
we were a little bit behind
there with iOS, with Android.

00:25:33.090 --> 00:25:36.690
There are new cool APIs
there to do new things

00:25:36.690 --> 00:25:38.910
with picking videos,
picking images.

00:25:38.910 --> 00:25:41.610
Now we have updated
that in.NET MAUI 10,

00:25:41.610 --> 00:25:44.910
and we're improving upon
that every service release.

00:25:44.910 --> 00:25:49.020
And then, of course, SafeAreas
and Material 3 enablement.

00:25:49.020 --> 00:25:51.210
The hybrid scenarios that I
mentioned a little bit earlier,

00:25:51.210 --> 00:25:53.010
there are cool new
things in there as well.

00:25:53.010 --> 00:25:56.910
For JavaScript interop, sending
messages through the bridge,

00:25:56.910 --> 00:25:59.400
right, from JavaScript
to C Sharp and back.

00:25:59.400 --> 00:26:00.840
Of course, tooling, right?

00:26:00.840 --> 00:26:04.050
We have cool new tooling
things coming up as well.

00:26:04.050 --> 00:26:06.570
For Android, already.NET
run support.

00:26:06.570 --> 00:26:08.940
I'll get back to that in
a little bit as well.

00:26:08.940 --> 00:26:12.900
Design time build speedups,
better VS and VS Code loops.

00:26:12.900 --> 00:26:17.070
And if you've not seen our kind
of like live property inspector

00:26:17.070 --> 00:26:20.340
and live XAML preview in Visual
Studio, definitely go check

00:26:20.340 --> 00:26:22.050
that out because it's
really, really amazing.

00:26:22.050 --> 00:26:24.390
You can just launch your
app through hot reload,

00:26:24.390 --> 00:26:26.070
through XAML live preview.

00:26:26.070 --> 00:26:27.570
You never have to stop
your application.

00:26:27.570 --> 00:26:29.580
You can just start building

00:26:29.580 --> 00:26:32.040
and everything will
show up in real time.

00:26:32.040 --> 00:26:34.680
The XAML investments that I've
just shown you, and, of course,

00:26:34.680 --> 00:26:40.260
the platform improvements for
Android, but also for iOS, are,

00:26:40.260 --> 00:26:42.960
you know, continuously
on our radar.

00:26:42.960 --> 00:26:44.280
And, of course, like, again,

00:26:44.280 --> 00:26:46.470
this is all AI
assistant engineering,

00:26:46.470 --> 00:26:50.760
which helps us take it from end
to end, reproduce, fix, test,

00:26:50.760 --> 00:26:53.160
write docs, do all
the things there.

00:26:53.160 --> 00:26:55.980
Hopefully, you have noticed
and that we have really picked

00:26:55.980 --> 00:26:57.600
up the steam, if you ask me,

00:26:57.600 --> 00:27:01.230
by delivering great MAUI
versions one after another.

00:27:01.230 --> 00:27:02.640
So then, what is next?

00:27:02.640 --> 00:27:03.960
What comes after this?

00:27:03.960 --> 00:27:06.840
.NET 11, the next
wave for.NET MAUI.

00:27:06.840 --> 00:27:09.810
Well, this is a big one,
and we tweeted this out.

00:27:09.810 --> 00:27:12.990
I sent this out,
and it's like one

00:27:12.990 --> 00:27:16.950
of those not very glamorous
things that are very visible,

00:27:16.950 --> 00:27:19.440
but CoreCLR becomes
the default runtime.

00:27:19.440 --> 00:27:25.860
So that means we, until now,
on iOS and Android and macOS,

00:27:25.860 --> 00:27:28.800
we were still running on
the Mono runtime, right?

00:27:28.800 --> 00:27:31.440
And a couple of people, whenever
we sent this out on the socials,

00:27:31.440 --> 00:27:35.160
were like, oh, I thought
Mono was already gone.

00:27:35.160 --> 00:27:38.130
So I like to think that
is because of how great

00:27:38.130 --> 00:27:40.860
of a job we did, so that
you didn't even notice.

00:27:40.860 --> 00:27:44.700
But now, we will actually
move to CoreCLR with iOS,

00:27:44.700 --> 00:27:47.940
with Android, with Mercatalyst,
also for.NET MAUI, and,

00:27:47.940 --> 00:27:49.770
of course, the other platforms
that I just mentioned,

00:27:49.770 --> 00:27:52.800
if you use those directly,
so that we have one runtime,

00:27:52.800 --> 00:27:56.070
one actual runtime for all these
things together with ASP.NET,

00:27:56.070 --> 00:28:00.150
with Azure services, with
other.NET workloads, so that,

00:28:00.150 --> 00:28:02.280
you know, we will
automatically kind

00:28:02.280 --> 00:28:06.660
of like get the performance
upgrades that.NET gets.

00:28:06.660 --> 00:28:09.120
You can start using the
diagnostics tools that are

00:28:09.120 --> 00:28:12.120
out there,.NET Trace,.NET
Counters are right now.

00:28:12.120 --> 00:28:15.090
If you have not been looking
into profiling your apps,

00:28:15.090 --> 00:28:17.880
this is a great time
to start doing so,

00:28:17.880 --> 00:28:19.680
and other tools are being
considered as well.

00:28:19.680 --> 00:28:23.940
So make sure, let us know,
again, feedback early on,

00:28:23.940 --> 00:28:26.310
what is important to you, what
you're seeing, what is working,

00:28:26.310 --> 00:28:28.230
what is not working, so
that we can fix that,

00:28:28.230 --> 00:28:32.100
polish that up before.NET
11 goes GA in November,

00:28:32.100 --> 00:28:35.970
and you will have the best
experience from day one.

00:28:35.970 --> 00:28:37.710
So that's going to be great.

00:28:37.710 --> 00:28:40.890
If, for whatever reason,
something does happen,

00:28:40.890 --> 00:28:44.370
you can very easily opt out with
a one-liner, but we expect this

00:28:44.370 --> 00:28:48.780
to be a very smooth transition,
and you will get only benefits.

00:28:48.780 --> 00:28:51.210
There is, at this point
in time, but again,

00:28:51.210 --> 00:28:53.790
it still previews
a little bigger.

00:28:53.790 --> 00:28:57.090
I think your app size will
increase by only a little bit,

00:28:57.090 --> 00:28:59.250
but you will get other things
in return, so it's kind

00:28:59.250 --> 00:29:02.460
of like a little balance there,
and we will work, of course,

00:29:02.460 --> 00:29:04.950
on this implementation,
if you will,

00:29:04.950 --> 00:29:07.950
to make it even more
optimized for you.

00:29:07.950 --> 00:29:09.360
So I already mentioned
it a little bit,

00:29:09.360 --> 00:29:14.940
the.NET run support, so no
more.NETbuild/Trun-Fnet 10.0,

00:29:14.940 --> 00:29:16.680
Android, iOS,
whatever, whatever.

00:29:16.680 --> 00:29:18.690
Just do.NET run.

00:29:18.690 --> 00:29:19.890
Boom. That's it.

00:29:19.890 --> 00:29:23.190
We have interactive
prompts right here to kind

00:29:23.190 --> 00:29:24.600
of select the target framework.

00:29:24.600 --> 00:29:26.910
If you just do.NET
run, there are options

00:29:26.910 --> 00:29:30.270
to specify the target framework
immediately, of course,

00:29:30.270 --> 00:29:32.790
but if you just do.NET run
and don't specify anything,

00:29:32.790 --> 00:29:35.730
it will ask you, like, hey, do
you want to run Android or iOS,

00:29:35.730 --> 00:29:36.750
or what do you want to do?

00:29:36.750 --> 00:29:38.820
And on which device, right,
you can see, like, hey,

00:29:38.820 --> 00:29:40.320
the little menu coming
up, you can switch

00:29:40.320 --> 00:29:43.950
between the emulators, all
the iOS emulators, pick one,

00:29:43.950 --> 00:29:46.110
and it will start it up for you.

00:29:46.110 --> 00:29:49.560
And this is what you probably
will see a lot more also

00:29:49.560 --> 00:29:53.100
in my slides after this, and
also in the.NET ecosystem,

00:29:53.100 --> 00:29:55.170
also in the world as a whole,

00:29:55.170 --> 00:29:57.030
that you will get
more CLI tools,

00:29:57.030 --> 00:30:01.770
because CLI tools are super
easy to invoke by Copilot,

00:30:01.770 --> 00:30:03.780
and they're also super
easy to kind of, like,

00:30:03.780 --> 00:30:06.210
parse the output from,
again, by Copilot.

00:30:06.210 --> 00:30:09.180
So this is very agent-friendly,
and I know there's a lot

00:30:09.180 --> 00:30:11.370
of terminal fans
out there as well.

00:30:11.370 --> 00:30:14.130
So you know, if that's
something that you like,

00:30:14.130 --> 00:30:16.320
then you can just use
this as the human pilot,

00:30:16.320 --> 00:30:19.830
but also it will be much
more efficient to use

00:30:19.830 --> 00:30:21.540
with the Copilot, right?

00:30:21.540 --> 00:30:25.980
So in line with this, we
also have.NET Watch support,

00:30:25.980 --> 00:30:28.980
and there you can
have a hot reload

00:30:28.980 --> 00:30:31.260
in a terminal implementation
here as well.

00:30:31.260 --> 00:30:34.350
So if you remember.NET Watch
from other.NET workloads,

00:30:34.350 --> 00:30:35.940
you can now just do.NET Watch.

00:30:35.940 --> 00:30:39.810
It will start up on your Android
emulator, on your iOS simulator,

00:30:39.810 --> 00:30:42.180
and whenever you start making
changes in the code here

00:30:42.180 --> 00:30:45.270
in the background, that will
be picked up automatically

00:30:45.270 --> 00:30:49.350
in your running application
in the running debug session.

00:30:49.350 --> 00:30:54.120
And you can see the results
right away and start working

00:30:54.120 --> 00:30:57.120
with those again, which is also
really great whenever Copilot

00:30:57.120 --> 00:30:59.220
wants to kind of, like, verify
things that it's working

00:30:59.220 --> 00:31:03.270
on without having to stop and
rebuild your entire application.

00:31:03.270 --> 00:31:06.690
So lots and lots of cool things
here from the tooling side,

00:31:06.690 --> 00:31:10.380
but what are we doing also
inside of the box for.NET MAUI?

00:31:10.380 --> 00:31:13.230
Well, we are putting
more pins on the map

00:31:13.230 --> 00:31:14.910
for.NET MAUI 11, right?

00:31:14.910 --> 00:31:17.310
So we have this maps
control that is implemented

00:31:17.310 --> 00:31:22.170
in.NET MAUI, and as.NET MAUI
does, we translate everything

00:31:22.170 --> 00:31:24.840
to what it's supposed to look
like, what's supposed to work

00:31:24.840 --> 00:31:28.440
on iOS, Android, Windows,
MacOS, whatever the platform is

00:31:28.440 --> 00:31:29.280
that you're running on, right?

00:31:29.280 --> 00:31:34.260
So here you can see the iOS
device that is using Apple Maps,

00:31:34.260 --> 00:31:36.240
and this also works on Android,

00:31:36.240 --> 00:31:38.520
and we now added pin
clustering, right?

00:31:38.520 --> 00:31:42.540
We have a map control that you
can just put in there, XAML map.

00:31:42.540 --> 00:31:44.520
On Android, it will
be Google Maps.

00:31:44.520 --> 00:31:47.010
On iOS, it will be Apple Maps.

00:31:47.010 --> 00:31:49.530
And it was like basic
functionality was there,

00:31:49.530 --> 00:31:50.670
but we were missing some things,

00:31:50.670 --> 00:31:52.680
and now we were finally
able to add it.

00:31:52.680 --> 00:31:54.630
So we have pin
clustering, right?

00:31:54.630 --> 00:31:56.130
So you can cluster
a bunch of pins.

00:31:56.130 --> 00:31:58.710
You can see the number
seven there in the middle.

00:31:58.710 --> 00:32:01.830
Those are seven pins, the
Copilot lab, and six more.

00:32:01.830 --> 00:32:04.170
So if you would zoom in, those
would kind of like expand,

00:32:04.170 --> 00:32:06.120
and you will see all the
seven pins right there.

00:32:06.120 --> 00:32:07.950
If you zoom out, you can
group them together.

00:32:07.950 --> 00:32:10.080
You can steer the
grouping behavior.

00:32:10.080 --> 00:32:12.990
You can say, hey, this is
part of a group with a name,

00:32:12.990 --> 00:32:15.000
and it will only
group those together.

00:32:15.000 --> 00:32:17.850
Custom pins, also, you
know, right now you had

00:32:17.850 --> 00:32:20.610
to do custom handlers,
which was, you know,

00:32:20.610 --> 00:32:22.320
it's easy to do in.NET MAUI.

00:32:22.320 --> 00:32:24.060
But if you don't have
to, that's great.

00:32:24.060 --> 00:32:27.630
So custom pins is now just
you specify an image source,

00:32:27.630 --> 00:32:29.460
and boom, you have a custom pin.

00:32:29.460 --> 00:32:32.700
You can see it down there with
little callout sign right there

00:32:32.700 --> 00:32:34.230
with Microsoft Commons.

00:32:34.230 --> 00:32:37.110
That is a custom pin that
has been implemented

00:32:37.110 --> 00:32:38.790
through this new API.

00:32:38.790 --> 00:32:41.070
With your interactions, you
can do a map long press.

00:32:41.070 --> 00:32:42.630
You can click on map elements,

00:32:42.630 --> 00:32:44.880
so you can see the little
polylines there, the circle

00:32:44.880 --> 00:32:46.320
and whatever that shape is.

00:32:46.320 --> 00:32:47.370
You can click on those

00:32:47.370 --> 00:32:52.140
and actually execute some
code depending on that click.

00:32:52.140 --> 00:32:53.190
And then, of course,
the clusters,

00:32:53.190 --> 00:32:54.660
you can also tap on those.

00:32:54.660 --> 00:32:57.030
More controls, those same
elements that I just mentioned,

00:32:57.030 --> 00:32:58.380
you can make them
visible, yes or no.

00:32:58.380 --> 00:33:01.380
You can play with the Z index,
so you can put them on top

00:33:01.380 --> 00:33:03.180
of each other and
change the order.

00:33:03.180 --> 00:33:05.010
And for Android specifically,

00:33:05.010 --> 00:33:06.870
you have the little
styling JSONs

00:33:06.870 --> 00:33:10.050
that you can now just
customize that styling

00:33:10.050 --> 00:33:13.230
from your Android map.

00:33:13.230 --> 00:33:16.260
Then other ways that.NET MAUI
is going to be more amazing.

00:33:16.260 --> 00:33:18.270
The first previews
are out right now.

00:33:18.270 --> 00:33:21.870
So again, I would love to
encourage you to test those out.

00:33:21.870 --> 00:33:24.750
Let us know the feedback so that
we can fix the bugs that are

00:33:24.750 --> 00:33:28.260
in there right now so that your
transition will be smooth.

00:33:28.260 --> 00:33:30.300
But here is a couple of
other things that are coming

00:33:30.300 --> 00:33:32.370
that are in preview right now.

00:33:32.370 --> 00:33:33.480
Less XAML ceremony.

00:33:33.480 --> 00:33:36.060
I already talked about that in
source generation by default,

00:33:36.060 --> 00:33:37.680
implicit namespaces by default.

00:33:37.680 --> 00:33:39.840
Better compiled bindings
diagnostics, right?

00:33:39.840 --> 00:33:42.000
Those are always a little
bit harder to track as well.

00:33:42.000 --> 00:33:43.980
Diagnostics are
going to be better.

00:33:43.980 --> 00:33:47.850
Xcode support, which means
writing code in your XAML.

00:33:47.850 --> 00:33:50.430
Some people have requested
that for a long, long time.

00:33:50.430 --> 00:33:52.710
So definitely go check
that out as well.

00:33:52.710 --> 00:33:55.350
Built-in long press
gesture recognizer.

00:33:55.350 --> 00:33:57.570
We now have badges
on shell tabs.

00:33:57.570 --> 00:33:59.430
So on your kind of
like your tab pages,

00:33:59.430 --> 00:34:02.130
you can have little badge values
just like the push notifications

00:34:02.130 --> 00:34:04.350
on the app icons on
the home screen.

00:34:04.350 --> 00:34:06.390
Same thing for the toolbar item

00:34:06.390 --> 00:34:09.360
and more implementation
of more cool things.

00:34:09.360 --> 00:34:12.240
Android-themed icons whenever
you have the home screen set

00:34:12.240 --> 00:34:15.630
to kind of like a monochrome
styling that can be done

00:34:15.630 --> 00:34:17.460
with your.NET MAUI
app right now.

00:34:17.460 --> 00:34:22.620
More permissions, trimmable
CSS so that it will have less.

00:34:22.620 --> 00:34:23.760
Your app size will go

00:34:23.760 --> 00:34:25.710
down whenever something
can be trimmed off

00:34:25.710 --> 00:34:28.500
and lots of cool other things.

00:34:28.500 --> 00:34:31.920
Now, lastly, putting
the AI in MAUI.

00:34:31.920 --> 00:34:34.380
There is a lot going on
in the world right now.

00:34:34.380 --> 00:34:38.010
So also, of course, in the MAUI
world that has to do with AI.

00:34:38.010 --> 00:34:41.490
But, you know, MAUI has
always been 50% AI, right?

00:34:41.490 --> 00:34:42.900
The letters are right there.

00:34:42.900 --> 00:34:45.840
So you know, we were ahead of
the game, I would like to think.

00:34:45.840 --> 00:34:50.790
And together with that, we came
up with a new initiative here,

00:34:50.790 --> 00:34:54.690
which is MAUI Labs because
things are going faster

00:34:54.690 --> 00:34:55.260
than ever.

